+## Prereleases
+
+Apps whose updates are GitHub releases can offer prereleases, such as
+`1.3.0-beta.1`, to the users who opt in: a beta channel. Set
+`prereleases` in `updates`,
+
+```ts
+updates: {
+  publicKey: "…",
+  github: "you/my-app",
+  prereleases: true,
+},
+```
+
+and `mygo build -upload` makes the draft of a version with a pre-release
+part a GitHub prerelease, which never becomes the latest release: apps
+that do not take prereleases never see it. Publish it with
+`gh release edit v1.3.0-beta.1 --draft=false`.
+
+The app chooses at run time, for example from a preference:
+
+```go
+mygo.Updater.SetPrereleases(settings.Beta)
+```
+
+`Check` then lists the releases through the GitHub API and offers the
+highest version among those that are not drafts, a release or a
+prerelease, when it is newer than the running one: a beta user gets the
+next beta, and the release that follows it. It is one API request a
+check, within the 60 an hour that GitHub allows each address; when the
+API fails, `Check` falls back to the latest release, and returns the
+API's error when that is no update. Updates never go back: a user who
+turns prereleases off on a beta waits for a release newer than it. The
+choice is not saved: set it each time the app starts, before the first
+check. A feed that has no release yet, such as the latest release's
+while only prereleases are published, makes `Check` return an error
+wrapping `mygo.ErrNotPublished`, which the updater plugin shows as up to
+date.
+
+[Delta updates](#delta-updates) of a build with `prereleases` come from
+the newest version of both channels, the latest release and the newest
+prerelease, then the versions their manifests list: a beta user on the
+last beta and a user of the last release both download a delta of the
+next version, whichever channel it is in.
+
 ## Delta updates
 
 As with Sparkle, apps download only what changed since their version,
